[Music]  las vegas firefighters gearing up for  one of the busiest weeks of the year  as the fourth of july holiday right  around the corner that means the use of  fireworks will be popular  all across the valley madison kimbrough  has the details on this year's  safe and sane fireworks hey sasha for  those that may not know safe and sane  fireworks have been tested and approved  by our city's fire department  to only be used during this one week of  celebration for the fourth of july  holiday  at all other times of the year safe and  sane fireworks are illegal as well as  any other type of fireworks that pop  off in the air the goal is for people to  buy these safe and sane fireworks and  use them responsibly  that means they should be used on a flat  firm surface such as the ground or  driveway  stay away from bushes grass or trees  always have a bucket of water and or  garden hose available for use in case of  a fire  and after the firework has been used it  should be picked up with a shovel  dropped into a bucket of water and then  soaked for several hours before being  put in the trash  tim cemanski with las vegas fire and  rescue explains the typical situation he  sees with people using fireworks this  time of year  what we commonly see is people sitting  in a cul-de-sac  and there might be a bunch of lawn  chairs around in a circle and  the people let the kids pick out which  one that they want  next and everybody's watching it  together that's probably the best way to  do it  another reminder keep vehicles in the  garage and all windows shut  as for our pets this is one of the  hardest times with the loud sounds and  vibrations  causing a lot of anxiety for them so  it's important we protect them by  keeping them indoors  and if you need to report illegal  fireworks you can use the website on  your screen that's i spyfireworks.com  only call 911 if someone is injured by  fireworks  reporting from las vegas madison  kimbrough 8 news now  and remember fireworks no matter what  kind are illegal on streets  in any parks and on school district  properties

